    "stdout": "---\nname: grit\ndescription: Use the grit CLI for version control in Git repositories — status, diffs, commits, branches, merges, cherry-picks, reverts, fetch/pull/push and tags — with JSON output for scripts and agents. Use when a task involves committing, branching, syncing with a remote or reading history and `grit` is installed.\n---\n\n# grit\n\n`grit` is a small, opinionated Git client. It reads and writes ordinary Git repositories, so `git` and `grit` can be used on the same repository. It does not mirror Git's commands or flags: there is usually one obvious way to do each thing, and every command can answer in JSON.\n\nThis skill was generated by `grit [VERSION]`. Run `grit <command> --help` for the exact options of the installed version.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Pass `--json` whenever you will read the result. stdout then holds exactly one JSON object; progress and prompts go to stderr.\n- Use `--filter '<jq-like expr>'` (with `--json`) to keep only what you need: `grit status --json --filter '{branch, clean}'`.\n- On failure the exit code is 1 and stdout is `{\"error\": \"…\"}`. Exit code 2 is a usage error (unknown flag or command) and has no JSON.\n- Always give `grit commit` a message (`-m`, a positional argument, or `-F` / `--message-file`, with `-` for stdin). It never opens an editor. Use `--author \"Name <email>\"` to override the author once; `--no-verify` skips `pre-commit` and `commit-msg`. Use `--dry-run` to preview paths, parents, and author without writing objects or updating refs (`--json` omits `oid` and sets `dry_run: true`).\n- Don't translate Git habits flag-for-flag. `grit commit -a`, `git add -p`, and `reset` don't exist here; use `grit commit --amend` to replace the tip commit and `grit rebase` for replay onto another base (see \"What grit doesn't do\" for the rest).\n\n## Where am I?\n\n```console\n$ grit status --json\n```\n\nPlain `grit` is the same as `grit status`. Keys: `branch`, `detached`, `head`, `target` (the branch your work is headed for), `ahead`, `commits` (up to ten not on the target; each commit row includes `change_id` and `change_id_stored`), `staged`, `unstaged`, `untracked`, `clean`.\n\nThe target branch is the first that exists of: the `target.branch` config value, `origin/master`, `origin/main`, `master`, `main`. Change it with `grit config target.branch origin/develop`.\n\n## Reading history and changes\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Uncommitted changes as a diff | `grit diff` |\n| Changed paths only | `grit diff --name-only` |\n| Per-file line counts | `grit diff --stat` or `grit diff --numstat` |\n| The change one commit made | `grit show <commit>` (patch by default; merge commits use combined diffs unless `--diff-merges=first-parent`) |\n| Several commits at once | `grit show <A> <B>` (`--json` → `{ \"objects\": [ … ] }`) |\n| Blob or tree at a revision | `grit show <rev>:<path>` (same resolution as `grit cat`) |\n| A commit's message and diffstat only | `grit show [<commit-or-branch-or-tag>] --stat` |\n| Recent history, ten at a time | `grit log`, then `grit log --before <next>` using the `next` value |\n| History with patches or file lists | `grit log -p`, `grit log --stat`, `grit log --name-status`, … (same format flags as `grit diff`) |\n| Commits on this branch not on the target | `grit shortlog` |\n\nRevisions can be full or short ids, branch or tag names, or expressions like `HEAD~2`.\n\nCommit rows in `--json` from `log`, `show`, `revs`, and `status` share `change_id` (reverse-hex) and `change_id_stored`. Human `log` and `show` prefix each commit with `change <8 chars>`; `--oneline` does not.\n\n## Committing\n\n```console\n$ grit commit -m \"Explain what changed and why\"\n```\n\n`grit commit` **auto-stages** the whole worktree by default (`commit.autoStage=true`). Use **`grit commit --staged`** to commit only the index (set `commit.autoStage=false` to make that the default). Use **`grit commit -m msg -- <paths>`** (or `--pathspec-from-file`) for a partial commit that leaves other staged changes alone — like `git commit --only`. `-a` / `--all` forces auto-stage; `--staged` and paths cannot be combined with each other or with `-a`.\n\n### Staging (`grit add`)\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Stage everything (modified, deleted, untracked) | `grit add` |\n| Stage only tracked edits and deletions | `grit add -u` (or `grit add --update`) |\n| Preview without writing the index | `grit add --dry-run` (combine with `-u` or paths) |\n| Record intent to add (empty index slot, no blob yet) | `grit add -N -- path` (requires paths; does not alter tracked entries) |\n| Stage an ignored path | `grit add -f -- ignored.log` |\n| Stage specific paths | `grit add -- path/to/file` |\n\n`grit add --json` returns `{added, modified, removed, intent_to_add, dry_run, count, staged}` with repository-relative paths. Use `--dry-run` before bulk staging to bound agent work; path lists are sorted and stable.\n\n`grit commit` fails with \"nothing to commit\" on a clean tree unless you pass `--allow-empty`. It also refuses a detached HEAD.\n\nEach commit gets a stable **`change-id`** header (on by default) that survives `--amend`. Disable with `grit config grit.changeIds false` when you need Git-identical commit objects. See the docs site section on change ids for derivation and testing config.\n\n## Branches\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| List branches | `grit branch` |\n| Create a branch at the current commit (no switch) | `grit branch <name>` |\n| Create at a specific commit | `grit branch <name> <start>` |\n| Create with upstream tracking | `grit branch -t <name> <remote>/<branch>` or rely on `branch.autoSetupMerge` |\n| Rename a branch | `grit branch -m [<old>] <new>` or `grit branch -M <new>` (force) |\n| Set / clear upstream | `grit branch -u <upstream> [<name>]`, `grit branch --unset-upstream [<name>]` |\n| Preview any branch mutation | add `--dry-run --json` (no ref, config, or reflog changes) |\n| Create and switch | `grit switch -c <name>` |\n| Switch | `grit switch <name>` (also `grit checkout`, `grit co`) |\n| Delete a merged branch | `grit branch -d <name>` |\n| Delete regardless of merge | `grit branch --force-delete <name>` (short `-D`) |\n| Predict merge conflicts (no repo changes) | `grit merge --check <branch>` (exit 0 = clean/ff/up to date, 1 = would conflict; `--json` → `result: clean\\|conflicts\\|…`) |\n\n`grit switch`, `grit merge`, `grit pick`, `grit revert` and `grit pull` refuse to run when checkout would overwrite local edits or untracked files. Preflight with `grit switch <branch> --dry-run --json` (checks only; reports `changed_paths`) before switching. On refusal, `--json` emits `kind: would_overwrite` with `reason` (`local_changes` or `untracked`) and sorted `paths`.\n\n## Combining work\n\n- `grit merge <branch>` fast-forwards when it can, otherwise records a merge commit. `<branch>` can be local or remote-tracking (`origin/main`). Use `grit merge --check <branch>` to predict conflicts in memory without touching refs, the index, or the worktree.\n- `grit pick <commit>` applies one non-merge commit to the current branch as a new commit, keeping its author and message.\n- `grit revert [<commit>…]` undoes one or more commits (ranges OK; newest first). Merge commits need `--mainline <n>`. `-m` / `--message-file` set the message for a **single** commit only.\n- `grit revert --continue`, `--abort`, and `--skip` resume or cancel a stopped revert sequence (same conflict model as pick).\n- On a conflict these commands update the index and working tree with conflict markers, exit 1, and leave merge/pick/revert/rebase state on disk. To inspect a side or recreate markers without resolving, use `grit restore --ours`, `--theirs`, or `--merge` on the conflicted paths (index stays unmerged). Fix conflicts (`grit diff`), stage (`grit add`), then finish with `grit commit` for merges or `grit pick --continue` / `grit revert --continue` / `grit rebase --continue`. Use `--abort` on the same command to cancel; `grit rebase --skip` skips the current commit.\n\n## Remotes\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Copy a repository | `grit clone <url> [<dir>]` |\n| List or add remotes | `grit remote`, `grit remote add <name> <url>` |\n| Download without changing your branch | `grit fetch [<remote>]` |\n| Fetch one branch or ref | `grit fetch origin main` (or `grit fetch origin pull/123/head:pr-123` for a PR) |\n| Shallow fetch controls | `grit fetch --depth N`, `--deepen N`, or `--unshallow` (same semantics as Git; mutually exclusive) |\n| Fetch and merge the remote copy of this branch | `grit pull` |\n| Pull only when a fast-forward is possible | `grit pull --ff-only` (exit **0** on success, **1** with `refused_not_ff` when diverged; branch unchanged) |\n| Fetch and replay local commits on top of upstream | `grit pull --rebase` (or `pull.rebase` in config; rebase conflicts abort atomically, exit **1**) |\n| Replay local commits onto another base | `grit rebase [<upstream>]` (default `@{upstream}`; `--onto`; `--continue` / `--abort` / `--skip` on conflicts, exit **1**) |\n| Publish this branch to `origin` under the same name | `grit push` |\n| Push to a different remote branch name | `grit push origin HEAD:refs/heads/<name>` |\n| Publish rewritten history safely | `grit fetch`, then `grit push --force-with-lease` (or `--force-with-lease=<rev>` for one ref); stale lease → exit **1**, `\"rejected\": true` |\n| Publish all local tags | `grit push --tags` |\n\nOrdinary pushes must fast-forward the remote. If the remote has commits you don't, grit rejects the push (exit **1**, `\"rejected\": true`); run `grit pull`, then push again. After `grit history` edits (reword, drop, fixup, squash), fetch and use `--force-with-lease` instead of `--force` so you don't overwrite someone else's work. `--force` overwrites unconditionally when you really need it.\n\nURLs can be `https://`, `ssh://`, `user@host:path`, `git://`, `file://` or a local path.\n\n## Bundles and linked worktrees\n\n| Task | Command |\n| --- | --- |\n| Offline pack transfer | `grit bundle create`, `verify`, `list`, `unbundle` |\n| Extra working trees | `grit worktree list`, `add`, `remove`, `lock`, `unlock`, `prune` |\n\n## Tags and config\n\n- `grit tag` lists tags. `grit tag <name> [<target>]` creates a lightweight tag at `HEAD` or `<target>`. `-m` / `-F` create an annotated tag; `-f` replaces an existing tag; `--dry-run` previews create/delete. `grit tag -d <name>` deletes one. `grit tag --verify <name>…` checks annotated tag signatures and optional `tree-sha256` claims (exit 1 if any fail); `--no-signature` skips signature checks.\n- `grit config <key>` reads, `grit config <key> <value>` sets, `grit config --unset <key>` removes, `grit config --list` lists. Add `--global` for the per-user file.\n\n## Staying non-interactive\n\n- HTTPS GitHub: run `grit auth` once in an interactive session (it uses a browser device flow). After that, fetch, pull and push use the stored token. When stdin isn't a terminal, grit won't prompt to sign in; it fails with a hint instead.\n- SSH: set `GIT_SSH_COMMAND='ssh -o BatchMode=yes'` so a missing key or unknown host fails fast instead of waiting for input.\n- Don't run `grit update` in automation; it reinstalls the binary.\n- `grit manager`, `grit upload-pack` and `grit receive-pack` are plumbing that speaks Git protocols on stdin/stdout. Don't call them directly.\n\n## What grit doesn't do\n\ngrit has no equivalent of `reset`, partial staging, interactive commands, or annotated or signed tags. During a merge conflict, use `grit restore --ours|--theirs|--merge` to compare sides or restore markers, then `grit merge --continue` (or `grit commit` for Git-compatible finish). Use `grit commit --amend` (optional message, JSON `amended` field) to rewrite the tip commit. For `grit stash pop` or `grit stash apply` with conflicts, use `git` to finish resolving. When a task needs something else on this list, use `git` on the same repository and come back to `grit` afterwards.\n\n## More\n\n- Docs for every command, with JSON field tables: https://grit-scm.com/docs/\n- Agent guide (output contract, exit codes, credentials): https://grit-scm.com/docs/agents/\n- All docs as one text file: https://grit-scm.com/llms-full.txt\n",
